Spring Boot offers several advantages that make it a popular choice for Java developers:

Auto-Configuration: It automatically configures your application based on the dependencies (JARs) you have on your classpath. For example, if it sees a dependency for a database like H2, it will automatically set up an in-memory database connection for you.

Starter Dependencies: It provides a set of convenient "starter" dependencies to simplify your build configuration. For example, including spring-boot-starter-web is all you need to build a web application.

Embedded Servers: It includes embedded servers like Tomcat, Jetty, or Undertow directly within your application's JAR file. This means you can run your application as a simple executable JAR (java -jar my-app.jar) without needing to deploy it to an external web server.

No XML Configuration: It encourages Java-based configuration over cumbersome XML files, leading to cleaner and more maintainable code.

Production-Ready Features: It includes helpful features for production environments out-of-the-box, such as health checks, metrics, and externalized configuration through a module called Spring Boot Actuator.

Getting Started: A "Hello, World!" Application 
Here’s how to create a simple web application that returns "Hello, World!" when you visit a specific URL.

Step 1: Generate the Project
The easiest way to start is by using the Spring Initializr (start.spring.io).

Go to start.spring.io.

Choose your project settings:

Project: Maven Project

Language: Java

Spring Boot: The latest stable version.

Project Metadata: Fill in your Group, Artifact, etc. (e.g., com.example, demo).

Packaging: Jar

Java: Version 17 or newer.

In the Dependencies section, click "ADD DEPENDENCIES" and search for and add "Spring Web".

Click "GENERATE". This will download a .zip file. Unzip it and open it in your favorite IDE (like IntelliJ IDEA or VS Code).

Step 2: Create a REST Controller
A controller is a component that handles incoming web requests. In your project, navigate to src/main/java/com/example/demo and create a new Java class named HelloController.java.

Add the following code to this file:

Java

package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    public String sayHello() {
        return "Hello, World!";
    }
}
Explanation:

@RestController: This annotation marks the class as a controller where every method returns a domain object instead of a view.

@GetMapping("/hello"): This maps HTTP GET requests for the /hello URL to the sayHello() method.

Step 3: Run the Application
Navigate to the main application file, which will be named something like DemoApplication.java. It will look like this:

Java

package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class DemoApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DemoApplication.class, args);
    }
}
Right-click on this file in your IDE and select "Run". The embedded Tomcat server will start, and your application will be running.

Step 4: Test it!
Open your web browser and go to http://localhost:8080/hello. You should see the text "Hello, World!" on the screen.

Common Spring Boot Annotations
Annotations are a key part of Spring Boot. Here are some of the most important ones:

@SpringBootApplication: This is a convenience annotation that combines three other annotations: @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration, and @ComponentScan. It's placed on your main application class.

@RestController: Marks a class as a request handler for RESTful services.

@Service: Marks a class as a business service component.

@Repository: Marks a class as a data access component (e.g., for database interactions).

@Autowired: Used for automatic dependency injection. It tells Spring to find a bean of a specific type and inject it into the current class.

@GetMapping, @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping: These map HTTP requests of different types (GET, POST, etc.) to specific handler methods.

@Configuration: Designates a class as a source of bean definitions.

@Value("${property.name}"): Injects a value from your application.properties file.
